While I understand Perry's reluctance to see these issues first
raised in public, I think on balance it might not be a bad idea.
First, if I were in your situation I wouldn't feel right simply
ignoring them.  More pragmatically: these arguments will eventually
be raised; they are probably known to a rather large number of
people.  The longer we all try to sit on them, the greater is the
risk that someone hostile to cryptography will control the manner
and timing of their first presentation.  Better David Friedman than
Bobby Ray Inman; better a law-review article now than a front-page
spread shortly before the vote on a crypto-restriction bill.
BlackNet has, after all, been discussed on cypherpunks, which is
read by several reporters.  As may this list, for all I know.

> People generally soliciting for, say, murder services, can likely be
> stopped in other ways -- like sting operations.

Problem here: anonymity.  Murder providers will be very reluctant
to have any traceable contact with their customers.  One might
perhaps hire a killing of a fellow LE agent, and then try to thwart
and capture the killer.  If murdercorp-assassin communications are
untraceable, the only obvious attack on the whole system is to try
to make life difficult for would-be assassins.  

Would there necessarily be murdercorp intermediates at all?  For
their security, they would prefer not to have any physical contact
with their agents, providing only information.  This could still be
very useful: information on the target's itinerary, armaments, and
security system, for example.  The assassin could buy this
information directly, of course, but there might well be benefits in
specialization: volume purchasing and greater contacts in the
information market, for example.  A client would also benefit in
dealing with an intermediate because most people have no reason to
become familiar with the assassination-services market.  A plausible
picture might consist of a free-floating pool of murdercorps, very
difficult to nail unless they screw up, and a distinct shortage of
reputable and experienced assassins.  The degree of the shortage
is the crucial unknown.

