Date: Sun, 03 Oct 93 23:01:26 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: free will: Part One -- definitions & brain/mind positions

Thanks to John Clark for his interesting posts on free-will.  I agreed
with parts of it, but as a 'believer' in free-will, I was prompted to
present some alternative views of my own selection.  This is rather long
really for a single post, so I've split it into three parts:

1) Definitions of free-will, and a look at alternative
brain/mind philosophical positions.
2) How could a brain actually have free-will?
3) The ethical aspects of belief in free-will 

Part One 

When people talk of "will" or "free-will", there are at least three
alternative definitions.  The very simplest version of free-will is
the form that we can almost all agree on -- those who do not feel as if
they are being externally coerced into acting against their own wishes,
are said to be acting of their own free will.  It's no minor feat to
attain even this basic form of free-will.  But the next consideration is
the possibility that all human action is predetermined at the physical
level.  If the determinists are correct, the 'will' is a mere illusion,
as we actually behave according to the motion of a bunch of particles. 
Even when no external coercion is apparent, internal constraints exist.
There is however the possibility that the brain is actively involved in
selecting behaviour, and under conscious control of the individual.  If
this is so, it can be thought of as a second form of will.

But even this may not be sufficient for a genuine, metaphysical
free-will.  If we lived in a single monotrack relativistic universe,
where all events, past present and future, exist frozen and locked in
place by the laws of physics, then it follows that each individual is
subject to predestination and has no freedom to avoid this.  But the
future would always be hidden from each individual.  A could not
predict the future and disclose it to B, since A needs to know the
prediction before being able to calculate the effect of its disclosure
on B's behaviour, such effect being an integral part of the calculations
which must be completed to obtain the prediction.  

We can dispose of predestination provided the many worlds interpretation
of quantum mechanics is valid, or the many minds interpretation, which I
favour.  As an individual's consciousness stream diverges, each branch 
plots a separate course across space, time and the multiverse.  Of
course, there is no way of magicking yourself across into a universe
where you keep winning lotteries, for instance, but this scenario allows
a further degree of metaphysical freedom.  So the ideal version of
free-will is more than just an "emotion" -- it's a _fact_.  I have to
admit that this third variation is not as important as definition two
above, but the third could have a role to play in providing for the
second.
   
By the way, it's interesting to note that according to operationalists,
all of these forms of "will" / "free-will" are meaningless concepts.
Even if you ask someone whether they did X of their own free-will, and
they reply, "Yes, I freely chose X rather than Y" they could be lying, or
could have been coerced into saying it.  And methodological monists and
determinists claim that introspection is an unreliable and unscientific
method for understanding human behavior, so one can't even apply the test
to onesself! 

Below is the first paragraph, as provided by the on-line Grolier's
Academic American Encyclopedia:-

will (philosophy) 
-------------------------------- 
In philosophical discussion, will is usually paired with reason as
one of two complementary activities of the mind.  The will is
considered the faculty of choice and decision, whereas the reason is
that of deliberation and argument.  Thus a rational act would be an
exercise of the will performed after due deliberation. 
The will has figured prominently in the thought of many
philosophers, among them the 19th-century thinkers Arthur
Schopenhauer and Friedrich Wilhelm Nietzsche.  Historically, debate
has centered on the issue of the will's freedom, a question of prime
importance for the analysis of human action and moral responsibility. 
--------------------------------

It then asks, "In the philosophy of mind, the question is whether
reasons in the mind are identical with or reducible to events in the
brain and, if so, whether physical events determine choices, decisions,
and acts."  So this relates to my second definition of 'free-will'.

The positions at the two extremes of thought on the brain/mind
relationship are filled by the radical materialist behaviourists and
the idealist vitalists.  Behaviourists claim that there is no such
thing (or process) as subjective mental experiences.  Now _they_ may be
just zombies, but this hypothesis is not universally applicable.  It's
falsified by my own experience, and, I expect, that of most humans.  The
extreme vitalists, on the other hand, believe that mind can exist
independently of the body as a "soul" or "spirit", (if the body exists
at all).  We have no evidence for this, though, and it seems to be just
wishful thinking.  Although we cannot prove that "existence exists" it's
better for us to assume that there is a material world as we suspect.  I
expect those who believe that they are just a "brain in a vat" or a
"hatstand" would end up with psychological problems!  Not to mention the
invariably lethal consequences of belief in an independent "soul".  So
these positions may be regarded simply as historical curiosities.

The panpsychists believe that all matter has a consciousness-like
quality.  They cannot explain how or why an electron is "conscious",
for instance does it arrange photons in clusters on its 'surface' to
represent various concepts?  How could a particle store memories?
Parallelists at least hold that only a certain class of matter can be
conscious.  But they maintain that there are no causal links from brain
to mind or from mind to brain -- the apparent synchronisation of
subjective to objective reality is simply a massive series of
coincidences.  It's too improbable, to be taken seriously by many. 

Epiphenomenalists can accept that the brain may act on the subjective
experiences of mind, but only in a one-way causal link.  Incidentally
this contradicts Newton's third law -- a one-way causal link denies
the possibility of an equal and opposite reaction.  Einstein said: 
"...it is contrary to the mode of thinking in science to conceive of a
thing ... which acts itself, but which cannot be acted upon."  But
according to epiphenomenalism, mental experiences are merely a by-
product of the brain, and serve no purpose.  So the evolution of
consciousness could not be explained in Darwinian terms.  It's much more
likely that consciousness arose because it served an excellent purpose.
Those animals that had it enjoyed a selective advantage over those that
didn't.  For example, if we feel fear we're likely to make more effort
to run away than if we are just automata. 

Identity Theory, or Central State Materialism, says that mental events
are just physical events described in another language.  Most of the
physical world is comprised of physical processes that are not also
mental; however a small minority are also subjective mental events.
This offers some further advantages.  Mental processes are real, and
they can have a two-way causal link with the purely physical world, 
according to the laws of physics.  At first sight, this is now
compatible with the Darwinian point of view, and mental events can
exist for a purpose.  

But the Identity Theory is a purely physicalistic theory, and so all
causal links must be purely in terms of physical theory.  The fact
that the 'mental processes' subset of the physical world can exert
effects on the rest of the world is irrelevant, as far as causal links
are concerned.  The 'mental processes' feature is simply redundant and
ignored, and the clash with Darwinism still exists.  I.T. can't explain
why mental events emerged.  They "just did".

But mental events have a purpose if we assume that intelligent, conscious
individuals such as human beings really do have the capability to control
their own actions.  The genes, at most, are able to provide their
survival 'machines' with advice on general strategies, but they have to
leave tactics and immediate actions in the control of the 'machines'. 
The cerebral cortex allows the modelling of alternative actions, and a
comparison of their relative values.  Once this reasoning has been
completed, the will then takes over and selects that which returns the
highest value.  Before this faculty arose, a terrible price regularly
had to be paid whilst learning which actions were best.  Errors could be
fatal.  With the advent of minds, a much greater economy could be
achieved -- natural selection would operate on ideas in the memetic
domain, rather than on individuals.  This intelligence in itself has
survival value, even if such animals were unconscious.  But the
_conscious_ animals, with the will under their control, had further
motivation to act purposefully to achieve their chosen ends.  Hence
such conscious animals enjoyed a double advantage over their
comparatively mindless rivals.

So, given that subjective mental events exist (the most basic of all
empirical knowledge), and in a material world we can only explain the
emergence of complex new orders such as consciousness in terms of
Darwinian principles (unless we postulate the existence of 'god(s)' --
which are ruled out by pragmatism, Occam's Razor, natural law and
various tests of logic), the only remaining credible position is that
of an emergent interactionism.  In Part Two I'll examine how this could
work.

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

