Date: Mon, 04 Oct 93 00:45:21 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: free will: Part Two -- how could it work?

Part Two: How could a brain actually have free-will?

In Part One I looked at alternative definitions of "free-will", and
considered various philosophical positions on the brain/mind
relationship.  My favoured selection is emergent interactionism.  But
how could this actually work?  I'm not going to suggest that a 'soul'
resides in the pineal gland, and controls voluntary action by coming
into contact with the 'vital spirits'!  We can look at some properties
of computers, and see how the brain compares.

An important feature is the hierarchy of different levels.  At the
physical level, any observed particles would be seen to comply with
the laws of physics, for instance.  At the level of the algorithm, 
operations would comply with the laws of logic and mathematics, provided
the machine was functioning correctly.  Now let's say a computer has
just been programmed to carry out some simple operation, like calculating
prime numbers one every five seconds, or maybe just flashing the screen
every five seconds.  Meanwhile, several teams have been given the task of
examining the conditions from various levels, and making predictions of
essentially what will happen in the next minute.  The physicists have to
try to measure the characteristics of all the particles.  Electronics
engineers have to measure the timing of pulses at the inputs and outputs
of each chip, and are given full schematics and spec of all devices and
p.c. board wiring.  Machine code programmers are given the binary code
and memory contents including CPU registers as at the start of the 60
second period.  Another team is given the source code, and current value
of variables and program counter.  One team gets given a program
flowchart, and told the current state and position.  And one team has
been allowed to watch the screen, and told what the application is.
Who would be most likely to make the correct prediction? 

The machine's very raison d'etre (at that time) is the process(es)
occuring at the very highest level.  We can make more reliable
predictions by examining the high-level operations rather than low-level
operations, and this also makes the predictions rather less involved. 
Although you would not expect to find any violation of natural law
through observation of any level, it's better to think of the highest
level of the hierarchy as the cause of what's happening.  What we have
is an example of downward causation.  Lower levels may interact with
higher level structures and functions, but also the higher level
functions may exert an influence on the lower levels, like an algorithm
arranging for electrons to be fired in a CRT every few seconds. A Watt
steam governor is another example of downward causation, where the
higher level structure of the machine applies negative feedback in order
to control a lower level process -- the flow of molecules of steam. 

Of course, downward causation can only apply in the case of physical
structures which are assembled to form a more complex order.  The higher
the complexity of structure, the higher the potential complexity of
function at the top of the hierarchy.  In some cases the total structure
is not any more complex than the individual units comprising it.  Take
the example of individual human beings, living together in a "society".
The top-down holistic thinking of neo-Classical/Keynesian/Marxist
economists and statist regulators fails, as the human brain is currently
the most complex structure, at least in this sector of the galaxy.
Purpose and causation will tend to flow from the higher complexity
structure to the lower level, so in this example a bottom-up
praxeological approach works best, with the individuals in control,
allowing emergence of a spontaneous "invisible hand".

If we accept that in the computer example it is more sensible to think
of the high-level function as the cause of events, isn't everything
really determined by occurrences at the microcosmic level as well?
Fortunately the Uncertainty Principle charges to the rescue.  The exact
world-line of a particle is unknowable, but probabilities can be assigned
to the alternatives.  I'll wager that if a high-level function required
the particle to select a specific option in order to make things work at
the higher level, the particle would "just happen" to select that option.
But we are provided with a useful buffer which partially decouples the
macrocosmic and microcosmic levels from each other.  Now the cause may
be either at the higher logical level or the physical level.

In discussions on free-will and the mind's capabilities, those who
believe that we're really just "robots" would make claims like:
a) The will or mind cannot cause any event, as this would require the
postulation of a "fifth force", and b) There is no qualitative
distinction between a massively parallel transputer and a single CPU
serial computer, they are only universal Turing machines.  Taking a)
first, it's interesting that many scientists believe in the Copenhagen
Interpretation of quantum mechanics, in which minds "collapse
wavefunctions" and inanimate matter is capable of "ghostly action at a
distance".  :-) :-)  The idea of a mind controlling part of the physical
structure that gives rise to it seems rather tame by comparison.
Anyway, I see nothing wrong with the principle of downward causation.
If matter is assembled together in a particular complex order, the
whole can become something more than the sum of its parts (to assume
otherwise is a fallacy of composition).  Matter assembled to form a
computer can routinely control the thoughts of dozens of humans halfway
around the world, for instance, and this certainly needs no fifth force.

In the example of a typical microcomputer running a simple application,
the machine certainly doesn't have free-will.  So how is a conscious
brain more than a micro?  Well, in several ways it's worse -- clock
speed about 1E6 times slower, and as a computer it's pre-von Neuman.
You can't just modify the programs by typing a few keystrokes, the
wiring has to be changed each time.  And backup is not guaranteed.  But
where it can score is with a massive parallel processing capacity, where
each of two million modules can represent a specific meme or concept,
enabling inputs to be very efficiently turned into concepts by parallel
scanning of open modules.  And the interconnections between these
modules corresponds to the actual relationship between concepts, like
a gigantic hypertext system.  Experience a sensation of the colour 'red'
for example, and you find it's linked with 'blood' and 'bacon'.  (Vegans
might not agree :-)

It's the global totality that is the key feature, though.  The human
brain is more complex than its constituent parts, and I suspect our
consciousness derives from the global brain rather than a specific
part.  You have to sever communication links between parts in order
to split the consciousness.  But wait -- according to my law of the 
flow of causation, the prevailing flow of purpose and causation is from
the higher-level complexity to the lower-level.  So the global brain
not only has consciousness, but it has control over some of its
constituent matter.  The consciousness has free-will!

In the case of the ordinary microcomputer, we'd assembled matter into
a rather complex order, and ended up creating a new higher order function
-- the ability to carry out data processing or mathematical and logical
functions.  I see consciousness as being a stage beyond running a simple
program.  So just like you can't run a program using a single particle, 
you couldn't expect to obtain consciousness from a single serial
program.  Allow a million programs to run and intercommunicate in exactly
the right complex order, create all the concepts and their relationships,
give it inputs and outputs, and a new higher-level autonomous process may
result.  

In Part Three I'll consider the ethical or pragmatic consequences of a
belief in free-will.

At the time of the Copernican revolution some debunking may have been in
order, but it's time now to call a halt to the debasement of humankind
by gloomy pessimists, and take our rightful place, as masters of the
universe.  We are the end-product of four billion years' worth of
evolution, machines which have become something greater than mere
machines, thanks to emergent interactionism.  

