Date: Sun, 3 Oct 93 18:16:43 PDT
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com (Hal Finney)
Subject: BLACKNET: The truth is thought provoking

About a year ago, there was an exchange on the cypherpunks list on this
topic.  Someone posted a message, through an anonymous remailer, arguing
that truly free cryptography could bring about an "Extortion Explosion".
The idea would be that with truly untraceable messages and digital cash,
extortion would become easy and safe.  People could, say, threaten to burn
down your business unless you paid them money.  Chaum has shown how
such threats could be made anonymously while still allowing money to be
received.

Worse, the extorter could even use digital signatures from other victims
to make his threat credible.  He could also hire henchmen to do the job
while keeping his own anonymity.  Perhaps he pays a few thousand dollars
up front, with the remainder after the building burns down.  His own
identity would never be known to anyone.

All in all, it was a sobering picture.  And the anonymity of the poster,
maintained through a Chaum-style remailing mix, punctuated his point.

I don't think there was any real resolution of the issue, although I
recall a bit of debate.  I do think this is an issue which we who support
cryptographic freedom need to consider.  If the brave new world we are
working to bring about is going to be a hellish dystopia, it might be
nice to find out beforehand ;-).

